Sanity check on orderings in a Dekker-style handoff. Two threads; each sets its own flag, then checks the other's.

```rust
// thread A (B is symmetric with FLAG_B / FLAG_A swapped)
FLAG_A.store(true, Ordering::Release);
if !FLAG_B.load(Ordering::Acquire) {
    // critical section
}
```

We've caught both threads in the critical section on Graviton3 under a stress test (~1 in 40M iterations) and, much more rarely, on x86. I know Release/Acquire gives me no store->load ordering, so it needs SeqCst somewhere. My colleague says one fence(SeqCst) between the store and the load in each thread is enough and is the cheaper fix; I'd have made all four operations SeqCst. Which is right, and which is cheaper on ARMv8?
